As part of the project, I created a Bash script called:

website-check.sh

The purpose of this script is to check whether my cloud-hosted website is online. The script uses curl to request the website and return the HTTP status code. If the website returns status code 200, the script prints that the website is online. If another status code is returned, the script prints a warning message.
The script is stored in this repository under:

scripts/website-check.sh

Script code:
#!/bin/bash

URL="https://arinmehla.info"

STATUS=$(curl -o /dev/null -s -w "%{http_code}" "$URL")

if [ "$STATUS" -eq 200 ]; then
    echo "Website is online. Status code: $STATUS"
else
    echo "Website may be down. Status code: $STATUS"
fi


To make the script executable, I used:

chmod +x website-check.sh

To run the script, I used:

./website-check.sh

The output was:
"Website is online. Status code: 200"
This script is useful because it provides a quick command-line method to check whether the Azure-hosted website is responding correctly.





What I Learned
Through this project, I learned how to create and manage a cloud server using Infrastructure as a Service. I gained experience with Azure virtual machines, SSH, Linux commands, Nginx configuration, DNS records, HTTPS testing and Bash scripting.
I also learned that server testing should use more than one method. For example, ping may fail because of blocked ICMP traffic, even when the website is still working. In this project, curl was a more useful test because it checked the actual HTTP and HTTPS response from the website.
Conclusion
This project successfully demonstrates the creation and configuration of a cloud-hosted web server using Microsoft Azure, Ubuntu Linux and Nginx. The server hosts a custom Classic Cars Showcase website and is accessible through a custom domain name with HTTPS enabled.
The project also includes a Bash script to check website availability and GitHub documentation that explains the setup process clearly.
